diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the simple_bgp lab reveals no network anomaly. The BGP session between router1 (AS 1, 193.10.11.1) and router2 (AS 2, 193.10.11.2) has been continuously established since lab startup at 03:14:33 UTC with a single connection count and zero drops ("Connections established 1; dropped 0" on both peers). The FRR debug log on both devices shows regular keepalive exchanges firing precisely every 60 seconds from startup through the entire observation window (03:14:33 → 03:21:35), with no session resets, no BGP NOTIFICATION messages, and no prefix withdrawals at any point. Both prefixes — 195.11.14.0/24 originated by router1 and 200.1.1.0/24 originated by router2 — are present as best-path entries in both BGP RIBs and are installed in the kernel FIB. Physical interfaces eth0 and eth1 are UP on both routers; iptables and tc show no firewall rules or traffic-shaping queues that could cause selective keepalive drops. End-host configurations are correct (pc1 at 195.11.14.2 with gateway 195.11.14.1; pc2 at 200.1.1.2 with gateway 200.1.1.1), and a 10-packet live ping from pc1 to pc2 returned 10/10 with 0% packet loss. The routing team's hypothesis of BGP route churn is a false premise: there is no route flapping, no instability, and no corrective action is required on any device. The network is operating correctly and no BGP session reset is warranted.
=== END ===